In this code snippet, the `PUT_InsertContract` method inserts an element `x` at a specific `index` in the `arrList` array list. Before and after the insertion, several conditions are checked. The `Debug.Assert` statement is used to check if the last position of `x` in the old and new lists are different, as indicated by the comment "@@@ last position of x in old and new lists are different".--------------------
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
</code>
Here is the reason for the code:
The statement `arrList.Insert(index, x)` inserts the item `x` into the list at the specified `index`. The old and new positions of `x` in the list can be different after the insertion, as the new item may cause `x` to be moved from its old position.

Therefore, the code checks if the last position of `x` in the old list is different from the new list. If they are the same, it means that `x` was not moved during the insertion. In that case, the assert statement `Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != New_arrListLastIndexOfX);` will fail, indicating an issue with the code.--------------------
Debug.Assert(!(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX));
</code>

